Found by the B016 lane while probing the fixture against the live game,
2026-08-30. PRE-EXISTING false negative; B016 made it uniform rather than
causing it. Deliberately NOT fixed in B016 — see "Why not in B016".

## Defect
A bare value name belonging to an anonymous (inline member) engine enum is
accepted by the LSP as a resolvable identifier. The game rejects it:

    (83,16) ERR : No matching symbol 'HyperTnL'
    (84,16) ERR : No matching symbol 'RenderPlaneReflect'
    (85,16) ERR : No matching symbol 'MinimalReadOnly'

The rule: a bare value name of an UNNAMED engine enum is not a symbol. The
enum has no name in the game, so nothing in source can reach its values
without going through the declaring member's type.

## Three sets — do not conflate them

  A = game rejects, LSP silent  ................ ~261 (upper bound)
  B = pre-existing, silent BEFORE and AFTER B016 ... 53
  C = newly silenced by B016 ...................... 208

A = B + C. Set B is the values of the 14 collision SURVIVORS (the anonymous
enums that happened to win their module's `or_insert` race before B016);
set C is the values of the collision LOSERS that B016 recovered.

Derivation of 261: 387 distinct value names across the 132 anonymous member
enums, minus 126 that ALSO appear in a named enum (where a bare reference
resolves legitimately in-game).

**261 is an upper bound, not exact.** It subtracts names shared with a named
enum but NOT names that resolve for some other reason (a Core global, a
builtin, a function). The exhaustive probe below would incidentally yield the
exact number, since anything the game accepts simply would not appear in the
error list.

## Evidence quality

PROVEN — observed in `Openplanet.log`, RemoteBuild probe 2026-08-30,
Trackmania + Openplanet 1.29.5, live Wine client, run twice identically:

  HyperTnL            Graphic::CGxLightBall::AttenuationType    (survivor, B)
  RenderPlaneReflect  Plug::CPlugBitmapHighLevel::Mode          (survivor, B)
  MinimalReadOnly     Control::CControlColorChooser::StyleType  (loser, C)

INFERRED, present in the same probe but MASKED — AngelScript reports one error
per STATEMENT, at the first unresolved symbol, and the fixture put three names
per statement:

  SlideNoControl          Scene::CSceneCharVisState::CharPhyState     (B)
  NotTimed                Input::CInputPort::InputsMode               (B)
  Download_WaitingSources Net::CNetFileTransferDownload::DownloadState (B)
  TimeHhMmSsMil           Control::CControlEntry::Type                (C)
  Text_Icon               Control::CControlButton::DisplayType        (C)

INFERRED, never probed: the remaining ~253 of set A.

Not in set A at all: `VCenter2` — it also lives in the named enum
`Game::CGameManialinkControl::EAlignVertical`, so a bare reference to it is
legitimate. Dropped from the fixture for that reason. Any narrowing MUST
preserve this case.

**The load-bearing fact: two of the three PROVEN rejections are set B.**
`HyperTnL` and `RenderPlaneReflect` were silently accepted long before B016
existed. So the rule holds across BOTH classes, and this is not a regression
B016 introduced.

## Why not in B016 (decision, carried forward)
B016's mandate was to stop DROPPING enums, explicitly not to start rejecting
more code. Narrowing the value scan is a tightening, and it cannot be scoped
to set C: the same call site governs set B, so fixing C without B would leave
the checker inconsistent for no principled reason. Taking B too is a
behaviour change with its own false-positive surface — 53 names that today
compile clean in the LSP would begin erroring. That is its own red/green plus
dogfood exercise, which is this ticket.

A Codex reviewer on the B016 branch proposed exactly this narrowing (filtering
anonymous enums out of `GlobalScope::has_global_ident`). It was REVERTED
there, correctly: it turned 5 identifiers accepted at 91a296c into fresh
`undefined identifier` errors with no dogfood pass behind it. That revert is
not a rejection of the idea — it is this ticket.

## Work
- Narrow the bare-value scan so a value reachable ONLY through an anonymous
  member enum is not a global identifier. Expect the call site to be at or
  near `GlobalScope::has_global_ident`; confirm all callers before changing it
  (the B016 lane's second review pass traced six).
- Preserve the shared-name case: a value that ALSO appears in a named enum
  must keep resolving (`VCenter2`).
- Members keep reaching their own inline enum's values through the declaring
  member's type — that path is legitimate and must not regress.
- Re-measure all five FP fixtures against a binary built from the archived
  base commit. Expect movement; every delta must be explained. Do NOT trust
  baselines quoted in tickets — see the correction note on B019.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)

The rule is testable at the LIB level against the type index, no game needed —
that is the regression test that matters:

Red:
- `bare_anonymous_enum_value_is_not_a_global_identifier`: each of the five
  probed names errors (three proven + two inferred-but-probed from set C).
- `bare_value_shared_with_a_named_enum_still_resolves`: `VCenter2` resolves.
- `member_path_to_an_anonymous_enum_value_still_resolves`: reaching the value
  through the declaring member's type is unaffected.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize):
- one name from set B and one from set C both error (the fix must not be
  scoped to the enums B016 recovered)
- a value name appearing in TWO different anonymous enums -> errors once
- a value name appearing in an anonymous enum AND as a Core global / builtin /
  function -> still resolves (this is the residual in the 261 upper bound)
- a named nested enum's values -> unaffected
- completion and hover do not offer the narrowed names either (consistency
  with the checker, and B016 already excludes them from those surfaces)
- `typedb diff` unaffected

## Acceptance evidence (the exhaustive probe — do this INSIDE this ticket)
Generate a probe fixture with ONE name per STATEMENT, one statement per line:

    void Probe() {
        print("" + HyperTnL);
        print("" + SlideNoControl);
        ...
    }

The one-error-per-statement rule is already proven, so every rejected name
surfaces on its own line and the line number maps back to the name. No file
per name, no compile per name.

Name list generation: for each of the 132 anonymous member enums take its
values, drop any that appear in a named enum — the same computation that
produced 261.

Rounds: unknown whether Openplanet/AngelScript caps errors per compile (not
hit at 3, ceiling untested). Sequence cheaply — run ONE batch of 50 first; if
it returns 50 errors there is no cap in that range and the remaining 211 can
go in one round. Worst case ~6 rounds at ~15s each (stage / RemoteBuild-load /
read log / unload / remove via `scripts/issue_repro_game.py`), about 2 minutes.

Acceptance: before the fix, all 261 are "LSP silent, game rejects"; after, both
reject. Keep the probe as a permanent fixture, and record the EXACT count it
yields in place of the 261 upper bound.

